  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CO- Inclusive Livelihood for Vulnerable</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The prolonged and complex nature of Somalia&apos;s emergencies is underlined by a multifaceted interplay of political, economic, social,and environmental factors&#x2014;such as drought, floods, conflict, and fragile governance. These factors not only continue to erode the social, institutional, and environmental foundations of Somali communities but also significantly negatively impact the country&apos;s resilience and efforts toward food security. This intricate scenario defines the current programmatic landscape in Somalia, demonstrating how these intertwined challenges contribute to the ongoing crises and hinder the development of sustainable solutions.

The Sool region to be targeted by this project, has experienced prolonged conflict with the Somaliland regional government, leading to a declaration of separation and the aspiration to become an independent state within the Federal government. The conflict&apos;s aftermath is evident in the number of people residing in IDP camps. In the pastoral regions of El Afweyn, households have been severely affected by an extended drought, leading to a significant decline in their livestock, which are their primary source of income and livelihood.The pastoral and agro-pastoral regions of Togdheer and Bari in North Somalia also face significant challenges due to a combination of factors, including conflict and insecurity, chronic drought, and weak governance and natural resource management. These factors have severely impacted livelihoods and increased vulnerability to environmental and economic shocks, particularly affecting women.

Expected Ultimate Outcomes:
Outcome 1: Improved capacity of households to implement effective disaster risk management and positive coping strategies to mitigate the immediate and future effects ofexposure to shocks.
Outcome 2: Improved capacity of individuals, households and communities to adhere to positive development pathways, increase productivity and adapt through climate sensitive approaches, despite exposure to shocks from both rapid and slow-onset hazards. (Shock Response Programme)
Outcome 3: Improved capacity of pastoralist, agropastoral, and peri-urban households, to engage in strategies for sustainable livelihoods and economic growth to enhance food security and resilience (Sustainable Livelihoods &lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt; Economic Growth)
Outcome 4: Transparent and accountable governance structures at the community, district and national levels established to ensure an enabling policy and regulatory environment for sustainable livelihoods and economic growth to enhance food security and resilience. (Transparent, Accountable &lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt; Inclusive Governance)
Outcome 5: Monitoring, Evaluation, Accountability &lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt; Learning systems are equipped with a data management platform, which forms the evidence base for informed decision making and strategic programming among relevant stakeholders (Evidence for Adaptive Management &lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt; Innovation)</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org ref="NL-KVK-27108436" role="4" type="21">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In 2004 Oxfam Novib set up a Liaison Office in Nairobi, Kenya in order to support the Strengthening Somali Civil Society Program, at the request of the EU. This request was based on Oxfam Novib&apos;s work in Somalia to date, but intended to be more widely directed atthe entire civil society sector and the specific context in which it is operating in a post-conflict country. The program started with consulting local organisations in Somalia and Somaliland to get a complete picture of the sectors and regions where they are active, the challenges they face and the relationship between civil society, local political structures and the private sector. A program was then developed to strengthen the institutional development of Oxfam Novib&apos;s partners and those of other Oxfams, as well as a range of new NGOs.
In the first year local consultants were trained in Somalia. They were then seconded to civil society organisations to build their capacity in financial and organisational management. Furthermore, workshops were organised on specific themes suchas human rights and the role of civil society in the Somali context. The Liaison Office is now going to support the expansion of the program, focussing on strengthening both organisation and project management, while facilitating more training in the fields of civic education, governance, human rights and women&apos;s rights, HIV and AIDS and child protection.
Over the years, the Somalia Country Office has expanded its intervention on short term humanitarian response as well as development development interventions. In 2015/2016, a decision was reached to move the Country Programme operation, which was largely in Nairobi, from Nairobi to Hargeisa. The country office has a small office in Nairobi , Kenya and large operations office in Hargeisa, Somaliland.</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Oxfam Novib</narrative>
